diff --git a/docs/topics/class-based-views/generic-display.txt b/docs/topics/class-based-views/generic-display.txt
index a24e8c5494..7922c12a57 100644
--- a/docs/topics/class-based-views/generic-display.txt
+++ b/docs/topics/class-based-views/generic-display.txt
@@ -1,3 +0,0 @@
-==================================
-Built-in class-based generic views
-==================================
@@ -5,4 +1,0 @@ Built-in class-based generic views
-Writing Web applications can be monotonous, because we repeat certain patterns
-again and again. Django tries to take away some of that monotony at the model
-and template layers, but Web developers also experience this boredom at the view
-level.
@@ -10,4 +2,0 @@ level.
-Django's *generic views* were developed to ease that pain. They take certain
-common idioms and patterns found in view development and abstract them so that
-you can quickly write common views of data without having to write too much
-code.
@@ -15,3 +3,0 @@ code.
-We can recognize certain common tasks, like displaying a list of objects, and
-write code that displays a list of *any* object. Then the model in question can
-be passed as an extra argument to the URLconf.
@@ -19 +4,0 @@ be passed as an extra argument to the URLconf.
-Django ships with generic views to do the following:
@@ -21,4 +5,0 @@ Django ships with generic views to do the following:
-* Display list and detail pages for a single object. If we were creating an
-  application to manage conferences then a ``TalkListView`` and a
-  ``RegisteredUserListView`` would be examples of list views. A single
